\newcommand{\Kstar}{{K^*}}
\newcommand{\Kempirical}{{K^*_{\text{empirical}}}}
\newcommand{\Ktheory}{{K^*_{\text{theory}}}}
\newcommand{\auc}{\text{AUC}}
\newcommand{\jsd}{\text{JSD}}

\title{Generational Depth Estimation of Recursive Synthetic Text:\\Measuring the Discrimination Boundary}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

% === Abstract ===
\begin{abstract}
Web-scale corpora are increasingly contaminated by recursive synthetic content, yet no method estimates a document's generational depth.
We measure the discrimination boundary $\Kempirical$ (the maximum depth at which adjacent generations remain distinguishable) using 15 statistical features and an OBD-LightGBM classifier.
Across five architecture families (1B--14B), three source domains, and four decoding strategies at $\theta{=}0.60$, $\Kempirical{=}2$ for general web text (8/9 model configurations) and $\Kempirical{=}3$ for structured academic text (arXiv).
A single generation pass suffices to render general web text indistinguishable from deeper synthetic content, a \emph{one-wash laundering} effect.
Generator-independent statistical features, computed via a fixed reference scorer, enable 80--92\% accuracy retention among continuation-mode generators, and cross-scorer validation is consistent with $\Kempirical$ being governed by the generation process rather than the scoring model.
Downstream, domain-appropriate $\Kempirical$ correctly predicts optimal curation granularity: when contamination causes detectable quality loss, binary filtering minimizes degradation most effectively for web text.
\end{abstract}

% === Section 1: Introduction ===
\input{introduction}

% === Section 2: Related Work ===
\input{related_work}

% === Section 3: Method ===
\input{method}

% === Section 4: Experiments ===
\input{experiments_c1}

% === Section 5: K*-Aware Data Filtering ===
\input{experiments_c3}

% === Section 6: Discussion ===
\input{discussion}

% === Section 7: Conclusion ===
\input{conclusion}

% === Limitations (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\clearpage
\section*{Limitations}

Our findings are bounded by:
(1)~\emph{Scope}: model scale $\leq$14B (5 architecture families), English text (C4, Wikipedia, arXiv), four decoding strategies; arXiv $\Kempirical{=}3$ is confirmed with five models across four architecture families but with reduced sample size ($n{=}2{,}200$ per model); subsampling analysis indicates $n{\geq}2{,}500$ suffices for 100\% reliable $\Kempirical$ determination (Appendix~\ref{sec:power_curve}).
(2)~\emph{Protocol}: all main experiments use prompt-chain, which isolates text-intrinsic degradation from training dynamics; real-world contamination involves both prompting and retraining, and retrain-chain yields $\Kempirical{\geq}4$ (Pythia-1.4B LoRA; Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}), so prompt-chain $\Kempirical$ may underestimate discriminability in mixed settings.
(3)~\emph{Threshold}: $\Kempirical$ ranges from 2--3 at $\theta{=}0.55$ to 1 at $\theta{=}0.70$; 8/9 configurations yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ (Mistral-7B: $\Kempirical{=}1$).
(4)~\emph{Marginal boundary}: Qwen-continuation's 1v2 CI lower bound exceeds $\theta$ by only 0.002; cross-scorer validation drops this to $\Kempirical{=}1$ (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}). LLaMA-3.1-8B represents the most marginal $\Kempirical{=}2$ assignment, with the 1v2 CI lower bound within 0.3 percentage points of $\theta$.
(5)~\emph{Feature set}: the 15D features yield a feature-set-specific lower bound ($\Ktheory$ may be strictly higher); neural probe $\Kempirical$ is model-dependent ($3$ for Qwen, $2$ for Pythia/OLMo).
(6)~\emph{Segment length}: $\Kempirical{=}2$ confirmed for 128 and 256 tokens (CI lower $> \theta$); 512 tokens yields point estimate $\Kempirical{=}2$ but CI lower marginally below $\theta$ ($0.5979$), indicating the boundary weakens at longer generation lengths.
(7)~\emph{Curation applicability}: the filtering evaluation is validated on two architectures (Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct and Pythia-1.4B) with consistent strategy ordering (Appendix~\ref{sec:pythia_filtering}), though absolute improvement remains small and effect sizes diminish at lower contamination ratios (10--50\%; Appendix~\ref{sec:dose_response}).
(8)~\emph{Family balance}: the Qwen2.5 family provides 5/9 configurations; though spanning three scales (1.5B, 7B, 14B) and two training variants (base, instruct), aggregate statistics weight this family more heavily than the four single-configuration families.
Additional limitations (cross-model scoring sensitivity, feature ranking variability at the 1v2 boundary, structural composition confound analysis) appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}.

% === Ethics Statement (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\section*{Ethics Statement}

Generational depth estimation is intended for data curation and corpus auditing, positive-sum applications that improve training data quality.
Our experiments use publicly available corpora (C4, Wikipedia, arXiv abstracts) and do not involve personally identifiable information.
A potential dual-use concern is that depth estimation techniques could inform adversarial strategies to evade detection (e.g., by calibrating rewriting to mimic earlier-generation statistics); we note that this risk is shared by all detection research and that the binary detection boundary ($\auc \geq 0.96$ at 0v1) remains robust.
Code and generated corpora will be released upon acceptance to enable reproducibility.

\bibliography{references}

\input{appendix}
\input{appendix_extended}

\end{document}

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/pipeline.pdf}
\caption{Generational depth estimation pipeline. Seed documents are recursively passed through a generation operator $G_\delta$ to produce a depth chain. Statistical features are fed to the OBD-LightGBM classifier for depth prediction.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:formulation}

A generation operator $G_\delta$ (LLM + decoding strategy $\delta$) produces successive generations $X \to Y_1 \to \cdots \to Y_K$, where $Y_k = G_\delta(Y_{k-1})$ and $Y_0 \triangleq X$ (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}).
This sequence forms a Markov chain.
By the Data Processing Inequality, $I(X; Y_1) \geq I(X; Y_2) \geq \cdots \geq 0$: the original signal degrades monotonically.
Together with Markov chain convergence, this motivates a finite $\Ktheory$ beyond which adjacent generations become indistinguishable.

We define the operational lower bound:
\begin{equation}
  \Kempirical = \max\{k \geq 1 : \auc_{k-1,k} > \theta\},
  \label{eq:kstar}
\end{equation}
where $\auc_{k-1,k}$ is the point-estimate pairwise AUC (Area Under the ROC Curve) and $\theta = 0.60$ is a data-driven operational threshold (motivated below).
Bootstrap 95\% confidence intervals (CIs; 1{,}000 resamples) provide uncertainty quantification: we verify that boundary-pair CI lower bounds exceed $\theta$ and non-boundary-pair CI upper bounds fall below $\theta$ (Section~\ref{sec:kstar_boundary}).

\paragraph{Threshold choice.}
We set $\theta{=}0.60$ as a data-driven operational threshold, chosen at the empirical discontinuity in the AUC distribution (Figure~\ref{fig:kstar_theta}).
A 10{,}000-iteration permutation test yields a 95th-percentile null of ${<}\,0.55$ and $p < 0.002$ for all boundary comparisons, placing $\theta{=}0.60$ well above the empirical null.
$\Kempirical{=}2$ is stable across $\theta \in [0.55, 0.65]$ for all but two marginal configurations (Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci_extended}): 1v2 AUC values cluster at $0.61$--$0.70$ while 2v3 values cluster at $0.55$--$0.59$, creating a ${\sim}$5--11\,pp discontinuity.
Only at extremes does $\Kempirical$ shift: $\theta{=}0.55$ yields $\Kempirical{=}3$ for two Qwen configurations; $\theta{=}0.70$ yields $\Kempirical{=}1$ across all configurations.
The downstream filtering evaluation (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}) uses the OBD classifier without re-optimizing $\theta$, avoiding circularity.

\subsection{Features and Classifier}
\label{sec:features}

Each document is characterized by a 15-dimensional feature vector spanning four groups: \emph{surprisal moments} (mean, std, skewness, kurtosis; 4~dims), \emph{surprisal derivatives} (mean and std of first- and second-order differences, plus first-order skewness; 5~dims), \emph{vocabulary diversity} (type-token ratio, hapax legomena ratio, self-BLEU; 3~dims), and \emph{frequency tail statistics} (frequency kurtosis, entropy, rare token fraction; 3~dims).
All features are computed from token-level surprisal values via an external scorer model (full definitions in Table~\ref{tab:s5_features}, Appendix).
Self-BLEU is computed within each CV fold using only same-fold, same-depth reference samples to prevent leakage; it ranks \#1 in 1/8 conditions (greedy decoding) but is absent from the top-3 in all 7 others.
Removing self-BLEU reduces mean $\auc_{1v2}$ by $< 1$\,pp across all models (14D $\Kempirical = 2$ for all 4 configurations; bootstrap CIs in Table~\ref{tab:selfbleu_ablation}); in deployment where depth labels are unavailable, self-BLEU can be replaced with corpus-level self-similarity or omitted with minimal performance loss.
Hand-crafted features enable cross-generator transfer without retraining and permit attribution of discriminative power to specific text properties.

We use \emph{Ordinal Binary Decomposition} (OBD), decomposing the $(K{+}1)$-class problem into $K$ cumulative binary sub-classifiers, each estimating $P(\text{depth} > k \mid \mathbf{x})$ via LightGBM (hyperparameters in Table~\ref{tab:s6_lgbm_params}).
OBD achieves lower MAE than multiclass LightGBM (e.g., 0.984 vs.\ 1.016 on Qwen-rewrite) while maintaining superior pairwise $\auc$ (mean 0.6095 vs.\ 0.4883 for ordinal logistic regression).

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

We evaluate nine configurations spanning five architecture families (Table~\ref{tab:setup}): Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (rewrite and continuation modes), Qwen2.5-1.5B (base, continuation), Qwen2.5-7B, Qwen2.5-14B, Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, LLaMA-3.1-8B, and Mistral-7B (all continuation except Qwen-rw).
Each uses 5{,}000 C4 seeds truncated to 256 tokens, recursively generated for $K{=}5$ iterations (30{,}000 documents per configuration).
Default decoding: nucleus $p{=}0.95$, $T{=}1.0$; decoding ablation covers greedy, $T{=}0.7$, and $T{=}1.2$.
Continuation mode truncates seeds to 128 tokens and extends to 256; rewrite mode paraphrases the full text.
Evaluation: 5-fold GroupKFold cross-validation grouped by seed document; pairwise $\auc$ as primary metric.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Experimental configurations for depth-chain generation. \textbf{Config}: shorthand identifier; \textbf{Model}: architecture and parameter count; \textbf{Mode}: generation strategy (continuation or rewrite); $N$: number of seed documents per depth level.}
\label{tab:setup}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Config} & \textbf{Model} & \textbf{Mode} & $N$ \\
\midrule
Qwen-rw   & Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct & Rewrite & 5{,}000 \\
Qwen-cont & Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct & Continuation & 5{,}000 \\
Qwen-base & Qwen2.5-1.5B          & Continuation & 5{,}000 \\
Pythia    & Pythia-1.4B            & Continuation & 5{,}000 \\
OLMo      & OLMo-1B               & Continuation & 5{,}000 \\
Qwen-7B   & Qwen2.5-7B            & Continuation & 5{,}000 \\
Qwen-14B  & Qwen2.5-14B           & Continuation & 5{,}000 \\
LLaMA-8B  & LLaMA-3.1-8B          & Continuation & 5{,}000 \\
Mistral   & Mistral-7B            & Continuation & 5{,}000 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate generational depth estimation on nine model configurations spanning five architecture families:
Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (rewrite and continuation), Qwen2.5-1.5B (base), Qwen2.5-7B, Qwen2.5-14B, LLaMA-3.1-8B, and Mistral-7B, all in continuation mode except where noted.
All experiments use the OBD-LightGBM classifier with the 15-dimensional feature set (Section~\ref{sec:method}), with 5{,}000 texts per depth level (generations 0--5).

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{$\Kempirical \leq 2$}{K*<=2} Across Model Configurations (C4)}
\label{sec:kstar_boundary}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Pairwise $\auc$ for adjacent depth pairs with bootstrap 95\% CIs and permutation $p$-values for boundary rows.
$\Kempirical = 2$ requires 1v2 $\auc > \theta{=}0.60$ and 2v3 $\auc < \theta$; CIs quantify boundary uncertainty.
Extended results (all models) in Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci_extended}.}
\label{tab:kstar_ci}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccccccc}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Qwen-rw} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Pythia} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{OLMo} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Qwen-cont} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Qwen-7B\rlap{$^\ddagger$}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3}\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\cmidrule(lr){6-7}\cmidrule(lr){8-9}\cmidrule(lr){10-11}
 & $\auc$ & 95\% CI & $\auc$ & 95\% CI & $\auc$ & 95\% CI & $\auc$ & 95\% CI & $\auc$ & 95\% CI \\
\midrule
0v1 & .9956 & & .9932 & & .9970 & & .9971 & & .9638 & [.961,\,.967] \\
1v2 & .6955 & [.686,\,.704]\rlap{$^*$} & .6539 & [.645,\,.663]\rlap{$^*$} & .6145 & [.605,\,.625]\rlap{$^*$} & .6112 & [.602,\,.621]\rlap{$^*$} & .6168 & [.608,\,.626]\rlap{$^*$} \\
2v3 & .5893 & [.580,\,.599] & .5514 & [.542,\,.563] & .5496 & [.540,\,.560] & .5609 & [.551,\,.571] & .5492 & [.539,\,.559] \\
3v4 & .5435 & & .5152 & & .5091 & & .5409 & & .5276 & \\
4v5 & .5340 & & .5136 & & .5045 & & .5141 & & .5085 & \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{11}{l}{\footnotesize $^*$Permutation $p < 0.001$ for all 1v2 comparisons shown.} \\
\multicolumn{11}{l}{\footnotesize $^\ddagger$Generator: Qwen2.5-7B (fp16); scorer: Qwen2.5-1.5B.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_auc_decay.pdf}
\caption{Pairwise $\auc$ decay across depth pairs for model-mode configurations (solid) and decoding strategies (dashed). The horizontal dashed line marks $\theta = 0.60$.}
\label{fig:auc_decay}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci} and Figure~\ref{fig:auc_decay} show $\Kempirical \leq 2$ across all 9 model configurations: 8/9 yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ (1v2 $\auc$ range: 0.6112--0.6955; all CI lower bounds $> \theta$; permutation $p < 0.001$), while Mistral-7B is the sole exception ($\Kempirical{=}1$; 1v2 $\auc = 0.594$, below $\theta$; Section~\ref{sec:boundary_cases}).
LLaMA-3.1-8B yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ (0v1 $\auc = 0.9225$, 1v2 $\auc = 0.6123$, 95\% CI $[0.603, 0.622]$; borderline: 1/5 folds falls below $\theta$; Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci_extended}).
All four decoding strategies likewise yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ (Table~\ref{tab:s2_decoding}).
The 1v2 spread due to generation mode ($\Delta = 8.4$\,pp within Qwen) exceeds the spread across models in continuation mode ($\Delta = 3.9$\,pp).

\paragraph{Scale and instruction-tuning invariance.}
$\Kempirical{=}2$ persists from 1.5B through 14B: Qwen-14B (1v2 $\auc = 0.621$ $[0.612, 0.630]$), Qwen-7B ($0.6168$ $[0.608, 0.626]$), and LLaMA-3.1-8B ($0.6123$) all exceed $\theta$, despite progressively reduced 0v1 signal (0.960 at 14B, 0.964 at 7B, 0.923 at 8B vs.\ $> 0.99$ at $\leq$1.5B).
A base-vs.-instruct ablation at 1.5B likewise yields $\Kempirical = 2$ (1v2 $\auc = 0.626$), indicating the boundary is not an instruction-tuning artifact.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_kstar_theta.pdf}
\caption{$\Kempirical(\theta)$ sensitivity curve for six configurations. $\Kempirical$: maximum discriminable depth; $\theta$: AUC decision threshold. All configurations yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ (dashed line). At $\theta{=}0.70$, all drop to $\Kempirical{=}1$.}
\label{fig:kstar_theta}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:kstar_theta} shows $\Kempirical$ as a function of $\theta$: web text $\Kempirical{=}2$ is stable across a 5\,pp window ($\theta \in [0.575, 0.625]$), with a gradual transition to $\Kempirical{=}3$ at $\theta{=}0.55$ (two Qwen configurations) and $\Kempirical{=}1$ at $\theta{=}0.70$.
The domain ordering (arXiv $>$ web $\geq$ cross-scorer) is preserved across the full $\theta$ range (Table~\ref{tab:theta_sensitivity}; Figure~\ref{fig:theta_sensitivity} in Appendix shows the complete model $\times$ domain $\theta$ sensitivity).
All four decoding strategies likewise yield $\Kempirical = 2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ (1v2 $\auc \in [0.687, 0.704]$; Table~\ref{tab:s2_decoding}).

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Generator-Independent Transfer}
\label{sec:transfer}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_transfer_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Cross-model transfer heatmap. Each cell shows 6-class ordinal accuracy when trained on the row model and tested on the column model, divided by in-domain accuracy (retention rate). Pythia$\leftrightarrow$OLMo: 90--92\%; Qwen-rewrite$\to$continuation: 61--63\%.}
\label{fig:transfer_heatmap}
\end{figure}

Continuation-mode classifiers transfer well across generators (Figure~\ref{fig:transfer_heatmap}; full matrix in Table~\ref{tab:s1_transfer}): Pythia$\leftrightarrow$OLMo retains 90--92\%, Qwen-cont$\leftrightarrow$Pythia 84--85\%, Qwen-cont$\leftrightarrow$OLMo 80--85\% (0v1 cross-model $\auc > 0.96$ for all pairs).
Qwen-rewrite classifiers transfer poorly (61--63\%), consistent with reliance on mode-specific artifacts rather than generalizable depth features.

\paragraph{Cross-scorer validation.}
Replacing the self-scorer with Pythia-1.4B or OLMo-1B preserves feature rankings ($\rho > 0.90$) but attenuates absolute discriminability at the boundary (Table~\ref{tab:crossscorer}).
Qwen-rewrite $\Kempirical$ remains 2 under all scorers; Qwen-continuation drops to 1 (1v2 $\auc$: $0.6112 \to 0.5923/0.5858$), consistent with its marginality under self-scoring.
Both alternative scorers agree on $\Kempirical$ per generation mode, indicating that $\Kempirical$ is governed by the generation process rather than the scorer.
For arXiv, cross-scoring with Pythia-1.4B preserves $\Kempirical{=}3$ for three of four generators (Qwen-1.5B, LLaMA-8B, Mistral-7B; 2v3 $\auc$ range: 0.608--0.632).
Qwen-7B exhibits a borderline decrease to $\Kempirical{=}2$ (2v3 $\auc = 0.599$ vs.\ $0.626$ self-scored, $\Delta = -2.7$\,pp; 3/5 folds above $\theta$), consistent with the Qwen family's scorer sensitivity observed on C4 and Wikipedia.
Bootstrap CIs (10{,}000 resamples) confirm $\Kempirical{=}3$ for Qwen-1.5B (CI lower $= 0.617$) and LLaMA-8B (CI lower $= 0.605$); for Mistral-7B (CI: $[0.593, 0.622]$) and Qwen-7B (CI: $[0.584, 0.613]$), the CI straddles $\theta$, indicating that their $\Kempirical{=}3$ assignment is sensitive to resampling variance (Table~\ref{tab:arxiv_crossscorer_ci}).
A three-way ANOVA (Generator $\times$ Scorer $\times$ Depth-Pair; $N{=}300$; Table~\ref{tab:anova_crossscorer}) confirms that Depth-Pair dominates variance ($\eta^2_p = 0.998$); Scorer is statistically significant ($p < .001$) but practically minor ($\eta^2_p = 0.192$), consistent with a detectable but small scorer influence on absolute discriminability.

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Feature Ordering and Generation Mode}
\label{sec:feature_degradation}
\label{sec:generation_mode}

Among continuation-mode models, single-feature $\auc$ rankings are highly correlated ($\rho \geq 0.85$; Table~\ref{tab:top_features}).
The top-3 features (\texttt{surp\_d1\_std}, \texttt{surp\_std}, \texttt{surp\_d2\_std}) are shared across all three families, revealing that autoregressive generation progressively damps surprisal variability; however, no single feature subset of size ${<}8$ reliably sustains $\Kempirical{=}2$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:feature_subset}).
A feature group ablation (Appendix~\ref{sec:feature_ablation}) confirms that surprisal (base) dominates on arXiv (single AUC $= 0.809$, LOO $\Delta = 0.031$); on C4 and Wikipedia, all LOO drops fall within fold variance ($\leq 0.007$).
Qwen-rewrite diverges (\texttt{freq\_entropy} dominates), consistent with its poor cross-model transfer (61--63\%).
Within Qwen, rewrite yields 1v2 $\auc = 0.6955$ vs.\ continuation's $0.6112$ ($\Delta = +8.4$\,pp); continuation mode is therefore recommended as the canonical condition.

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Neural Probe}
\label{sec:neural_probe}

Replacing the 15D features with frozen encoder hidden states (MLP probe; Table~\ref{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}), Qwen achieves $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 3$ (2v3 $\auc = 0.6159$), while Pythia and OLMo remain at $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 2$ ($\auc_{2v3}$: 0.5963, 0.5892).
The Qwen-only elevation reflects self-familiarity artifacts that transfer poorly (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}); the 15D features yield consistent $\Kempirical = 2$ across all families.

